# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  NOTHING IS PUBLISHED UNLESS IT IS NAMED BELOW.
#
#  This file works the opposite way round from a normal .gitignore. Instead of
#  listing what to leave out, it excludes everything and then names the few
#  things allowed back in.
#
#  That direction is deliberate. A list of exclusions has to be updated every
#  time a file is added, and the day somebody forgets is the day something
#  private is published - permanently, because a public git history cannot be
#  taken back. With this direction, forgetting means a file is NOT published,
#  which is the safe way to be wrong.
#
#  Published: the read-me, the changelogs, the licence, and whatever is put in
#  release/.
#  Not published: source, notes, plans, tooling, audits, screenshots, the
#  question bank, build scripts, and anything else on this disk.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Everything.
/*

# The exceptions, and there are only these.
!/.gitignore
!/README.md
!/CHANGELOG_*.md
!/LICENSE
!/release/

# ── release/ ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The build script puts the current release's jar and its notes here, and
# nothing else goes in. Keeping them in a folder of their own means the
# allow-list above never has to name a file that changes with every version.
#
# Do NOT put the sources jar or the source zip in here. Both hold the entire
# source tree.

# ── A note on the licence ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LICENSE is published on purpose, and it is the one exception worth arguing
# for. The terms are all rights reserved, with one permission written into
# them: modpacks may include this mod, unmodified, without asking.
#
# That permission only works if people can read it. A pack author deciding
# what to include skips anything whose permission is unclear more readily than
# anything merely restrictive, and a repository holding a jar and no terms is
# as unclear as it gets. Hiding the file would work against the exact reason
# it says what it says.
#
# The source is not published and the licence does not ask for it to be.

# ── A note on history ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This file governs what is committed from now on. It does NOT remove anything
# that was committed before it existed. If a previous push put source, notes or
# tooling on the remote, all of that is still in the history and still public,
# and only rewriting that history removes it.

# ── Secrets ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every one of these is already excluded by the /* above. They are named again
# anyway, and the redundancy is the point.
#
# secrets.properties holds the crash-report webhook. A webhook URL is a live
# credential: anyone holding it can post into that channel. It is the one file
# here whose exposure cannot be undone - a credential pushed to a public
# repository is public from that moment, and replacing it is the only remedy.
#
# So it does not rely on a rule that has to be reasoned about. "Everything is
# excluded, therefore this is excluded" is correct, and it is one careless
# allow-list entry away from not being correct. A line with the filename on it
# survives that. The build refuses to run without these lines for the same
# reason, and it was right to.
secrets.properties
*.secrets.properties
secrets.local.properties
